---
import type { CollectionEntry } from "astro:content";
import { getPublishedCollection } from "astro-course-university/content";
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import { withBase } from "astro-theme-university/url";
import SequenceDiagram from "../components/sketch/SequenceDiagram.astro";
import StateMachine from "../components/sketch/StateMachine.astro";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { labMeeting, lectureMeeting } from "../data/timetable";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";

const lectures = (await getPublishedCollection("lectures")).sort((a, b) => a.data.week - b.data.week);
const readingCount = (await getPublishedCollection("readings")).length;
const parts: { name: string; summary: string; weeks: CollectionEntry<"lectures">[] }[] = [
  { name: "I. Read the protocol", summary: "Write down what everyone runs and nobody describes.", weeks: lectures.slice(0, 4) },
  { name: "II. Implement selectively", summary: "Run parts of it on purpose, and count what they cost.", weeks: lectures.slice(4, 7) },
  { name: "III. Negotiate", summary: "Institutions, closeness, disclosure, and other implementations.", weeks: lectures.slice(7, 11) },
  { name: "IV. Horizon", summary: "What the course leaves out, and where to read further.", weeks: lectures.slice(11) },
];
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <p class="home-meta">
    On campus it's NT 101. {courseMeta.session}, {courseMeta.year}. Lectures
    {` ${lectureMeeting.day} ${lectureMeeting.start}–${lectureMeeting.end}`}, labs
    {` ${labMeeting.day} ${labMeeting.start}–${labMeeting.end}`}, on
    the <a href={withBase("/timetable/")}>timetable</a>.
  </p>

  <div class="home-hero">
    <blockquote class="home-thesis">
      <p>The protocol is undocumented, not obvious; a minority implementation is not a broken one.</p>
    </blockquote>
    <SequenceDiagram
      actors={["A", "B"]}
      seed="home handshake"
      caption="The opening handshake, as week 1 specifies it. The reply is a token, not an answer."
      messages={[
        { from: 0, to: 1, label: "How are you?" },
        { from: 1, to: 0, label: "Good, you?", note: "not a status report", gold: true },
        { from: 0, to: 1, label: "Yeah, good." },
      ]}
    />
  </div>

  <h2>What this course is</h2>
  <p>
    The social protocol of the neurotypical majority is a large, undocumented,
    inconsistently implemented legacy system. The people who run it can't
    describe it, and none of them designed it. For twelve weeks, SLOP1562
    reverse-engineers it: a specification of the handshake, the hint, the pause,
    the apology and the email to your tutor, so that anyone can interoperate with
    it on purpose.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every week ends with scripts you can say, timings you can check, and lines
    you can test yourself against.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Anyone who wants the protocol written down. Autistic and otherwise
    divergent students, who were never handed the implicit version. And students
    who run the protocol fluently and couldn't describe a line of it, which is a
    reason to enrol in its own right. The class is mixed on purpose; week 11
    explains why that matters.
  </p>
  <p>
    Nobody is here because something is wrong with them. Entry is by three
    references from people who know you well. <strong>No diagnosis is required,
    and none is accepted as evidence.</strong> The <a href={withBase("/policies/")}>policies page</a> has the details.
  </p>

  <h2>What you'll do</h2>
  <p>
    A lecture writes a part of the protocol down, and a lab tests that writing
    in the room. From week 2, a weekly journal entry checks it against a real
    exchange of your own.
  </p>
  <StateMachine
    seed="home weekly loop"
    caption="From week 2: lecture, lab and journal. An observation can send you back to revise the rule."
    states={["Tue lecture", "Thu lab", "Sun journal"]}
    transitions={[
      { from: 0, to: 1, label: "test it in the room" },
      { from: 1, to: 2, label: "one real exchange" },
      { from: 2, to: 0, label: "revise", gold: true },
    ]}
  />
  <p>And three pieces of work that draw on the loop:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>In week 6, a <strong>Protocol Specification</strong> of one subroutine, with conformance tests.</li>
    <li>Over weeks 9 to 11, an <strong>Interoperation Report</strong>, negotiated with a partner.</li>
    <li>To finish, a <strong>Personal Protocol Document</strong> or a <strong>Field Experiment</strong>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The twelve weeks</h2>
  <div class="home-parts">
    {
      parts.map((part) => (
        <section aria-label={part.name}>
          <h3>{part.name}</h3>
          <p>{part.summary}</p>
          <ol>
            {part.weeks.map((lecture) => (
              <li value={lecture.data.week}>
                <a href={withBase(`/lectures/${lecture.id}/`)}>{lecture.data.title}</a>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ol>
        </section>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <h2>What you'll leave able to do</h2>
  <ol class="home-outcomes">
    {courseMeta.learningOutcomes?.map((outcome) => <li>{outcome}</li>)}
  </ol>
  <p>
    The outcome is fluency plus choice, without having to become
    indistinguishable from anybody. You'll be able to run a part of the protocol
    when it's worth the cost, and decline it knowing what declining costs.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title="Week 1" href="/lectures/week-01/">
      <p>Start where the course does: "how are you?", specified.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Timetable" href="/timetable/">
      <p>Where to be, when, and the room rules nobody writes down.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>Twelve Thursdays, each with a run sheet and timings.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>Four tasks, what each is worth, and every deadline.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Readings" href="/readings/">
      <p>{readingCount} sources, with notes on what was checked.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Glossary" href="/glossary/">
      <p>The vocabulary the class builds, and the week each term arrives.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches the course, and who to ask about what.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .home-meta {
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    font-family: var(--at-font-mono);
    font-size: var(--at-font-size-sm);
  }

  .home-hero {
    margin-block: var(--at-spacing-xl) var(--at-spacing-2xl);
  }

  .home-thesis {
    margin: 0 0 var(--at-spacing-md);
    max-width: 34rem;
    color: var(--at-heading);
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    font-weight: 400;
    line-height: 1.35;
  }

  .home-thesis::before {
    display: none;
  }

  .home-thesis p {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .home-parts {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: var(--at-spacing-md) var(--at-spacing-xl);
  }

  @media (min-width: 48rem) {
    .home-parts {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    }
  }

  .home-parts h3 {
    margin-block-end: 0.2rem;
  }

  .home-parts p {
    margin-block: 0 0.4rem;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
  }

  .home-outcomes li {
    margin-block: 0.4rem;
  }
</style>
